timeline/move-the-playhead [howto]: Previous and Next Edit Point, Go to Start and End, the timecode field and typing a time to jump, and the duration readout. Hopping between markers is on timeline/markers.
timeline/markers [feature]: Adding, naming, colouring, moving and removing markers, next and previous marker, the Document tab's Markers list with Go and Delete, the Chapter box, and markers while a nest is open. How chapters reach a file is on export/chapte
canvas/picture-tab [overview]: Every section of the Clip tab's Picture sub-tab in order, which clips get which section, and where each is documented.
canvas/corner-radius [feature]: Rounding a picture's corners, its range and which clips take it. A rectangle shape's radius is on text-and-shapes/shape-settings.
text-and-shapes/fonts [concept]: Which fonts text clips can use: installed fonts and the faces the app ships with. Fonts inside graphics are on graphics/fonts-and-pictures.
text-and-shapes/image-clips [feature]: Still pictures on the timeline: placing one, its default length, what it can be trimmed to, and which Picture sections it takes. Picture formats are on reference/file-formats.
captions/on-device-transcription [feature]: The on-device engine: its languages, downloading a speech language and its progress, the pause note, no cache, and asking for no permission.
captions/assemblyai-transcription [feature]: The cloud engine: Speakers, Vocabulary, language detection, the price shown before Generate, one transcript cached per media and reused with no key, Transcribe again, and partial failures. What is uploaded is on captions/assemblya
captions/assemblyai-privacy [concept]: What is uploaded and billed (the whole file), the transcript deleted after reading, automatic delete and retry, unconfirmed deletes holding a file back, and the warnings in the run report.
captions/transcript-tab [overview]: View ▸ Transcript: every heard word in timeline order, paragraphs with timecodes, speaker changes, notes for clips with no words, and the bar's Words from, Language and Read and Stop.
captions/transcript-words [concept]: An AssemblyAI transcript first, otherwise reading on this Mac one file at a time, playhead first; per-media cache; the missing speech model; ramped clips and following edits.
captions/follow-the-transcript [feature]: The highlight during playback, the text scrolling, clicking a word or timecode to seek, a selected clip scrolling to its words, and Loop playing the selected words.
captions/shorten-pauses [feature]: Pause tokens in the transcript, selecting one, Delete Pause, and how pauses are found.
captions/transcript-keys [reference]: Word and line navigation, extending and clearing a selection, select all, the Delete key, and transport keys while the text has focus.
graphics/what-is-a-graphic [concept]: A module drawn per frame: resolution independent, clips sharing one module, the project's graphics folder, and templates being copied in.
graphics/share-a-graphic [feature]: Exporting and importing a .fjgraphic: the browser's Export…, From File…, opening one from the Finder, the Import Graphic sheet, package checks, name clashes, and what a package finds to pack.
graphics/fonts-and-pictures [feature]: File ▸ Add Font to Graphics… and Add Image to Graphics…, dropping them in the folder, bundled fonts, exact family names, picture names made legal, and system fonts never being used.
reference/file-locations [reference]: The Application Support folder, recordings, rewrapped copies, key files and how keys are stored, keys from the environment, and the spend ledger.
reference/file-formats [reference]: What Frame Job reads, by movie, sound and picture: containers and codecs (AV1 and VP9 decoding included), alpha, portrait and rotated movies, variable frame rates, any sample rate, MP3 gapless start, and deep and HDR stills. Expor
reference/framejob-package [reference]: What a package holds: project.json, media/, graphics/ and cache/, what can be rebuilt, and how it travels.
reference/limits [reference]: Every hard number in one place: clips, tracks, markers, media and nest depth, then each area's ceilings, linking to the feature that sets them.
